{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Knot.RivinConcavitySimplex","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Knot.RivinConcavitySimplex","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Knot.RivinConcavity","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.StrictConcaveOnSegments","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.ConcaveOnSum"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.Rivin.strictConcaveOn_totalVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.Rivin.strictConcaveOn_totalVol","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RivinConcavitySimplex.lean#L121-L174","doc":"**RIVIN CONCAVITY FOR AN IDEAL TRIANGULATION.**\n\nThe total volume is strictly concave on **any** convex set of angle assignments whose\npoints are tetrahedron-wise admissible.\n\nThe gluing equations never appear. That is the point: they are linear, so whatever\npolytope they cut out is convex, and `P` may be taken to be it. Concavity does not care\n*which* convex set — only that it is one, and that each of its points gives every\ntetrahedron a genuine angle triple. So this covers Rivin's angle-structure polytope, any\nface of it, and any further linear restriction, without proving anything about the\ngluing equations themselves.\n\n**Where strictness comes from.** Each summand `tetVol ∘ (eval i)` is constant in every\n*other* tetrahedron's angles, so as a function on `P` it is concave and not strictly so.\nBut two distinct angle assignments must differ at some tetrahedron `j`, and there the\nsummand is strictly concave by `strictConcaveOn_tetVol`. `StrictConcaveOn.sum_of_mem` is\nshaped for exactly this: one strict summand carries the sum.\n\nThis is the variational statement the hyperbolic structure is characterised by — the\ncomplete structure is the unique critical point — and hence what the volume-conjecture\nwork rests on downstream. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.Rivin.totalVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.Rivin.totalVol","kind":"def","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RivinConcavitySimplex.lean#L116-L119","doc":"The total volume of an ideal triangulation with `n` tetrahedra, as a function of the\ntuple of angle triples. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.Rivin.strictConcaveOn_tetVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.Rivin.strictConcaveOn_tetVol","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RivinConcavitySimplex.lean#L74-L112","doc":"**RIVIN CONCAVITY FOR ONE IDEAL TETRAHEDRON, on the simplex.**\n\n`Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)` is strictly concave on `{α, β, γ > 0, α + β + γ = π}`.\n\nThis is the statement `strictConcaveOn_tetVolLine`'s docstring claims to be equivalent\nto; here it is derived from it, by `strictConcaveOn_of_segments`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.Rivin.convex_tetAngleSimplex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.Rivin.convex_tetAngleSimplex","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RivinConcavitySimplex.lean#L59-L72","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.Rivin.tetVol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.Rivin.tetVol","kind":"def","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RivinConcavitySimplex.lean#L54-L57","doc":"The volume functional on the angle simplex, `Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)`. This is the\n`tetVolLine` of `RivinConcavity` with the line parametrisation stripped off. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.Rivin.tetAngleSimplex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.Rivin.tetAngleSimplex","kind":"def","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/RivinConcavitySimplex.lean#L49-L52","doc":"The angle simplex of one ideal tetrahedron: three positive dihedral angles\nsumming to `π`. Points are `(α, β, γ)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}